INSERT OR IGNORE INTO stories (id, slug, lane, type, section_slug, status, sensitive, headline, dek, body, byline, hero_image_url, hero_image_credit, hero_image_rights, source_system, source_ref, priority, is_lead_candidate, created_by, created_at, updated_at) VALUES ('bd-20261123-uk-eu-summit-reset-deal', 'uk-eu-summit-brussels-burnham-food-checks-carbon-market-youth-scheme-deal', 'newsroom', 'news_story', 'uk', 'EDITORIAL_REVIEW', 0, 'UK and EU sign Brexit reset deals on food checks, carbon markets and youth visas', 'Prime Minister Andy Burnham called the Brussels package "a win-win," but the three deals will not start until 2028 and are on hold until a dispute over student fees is settled.', 'Britain and the European Union signed three agreements at their second summit in Brussels on Friday, Nov. 20, changing key parts of the post-Brexit arrangements, Irish broadcaster RTÉ reported. Prime Minister Andy Burnham met European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen and European Council President António Costa, according to the Council of the EU.

## What was agreed

**Food and farm trade.** The deals create a common sanitary and phytosanitary (SPS) area for animals, plants and food products. Trade in goods such as sausages and seeds would no longer need the certificates and checks required today, RTÉ reported. Britain estimates the change could add up to £5.1 billion a year to its economy. Most food checks on goods going from Great Britain to Northern Ireland would also end, though customs paperwork for food and drink firms stays.

**Carbon markets.** The British and EU emissions trading systems will be linked. Goods such as steel would be exempt from each side''s border carbon charges, according to RTÉ.

**Youth mobility.** Young people from each side will be able to live and work or study in the other for up to three years. EU diplomats told RTÉ that Britain had proposed 50,000 places but agreed to 100,000.

## The catch: student fees and timing

A deal on university tuition fees was postponed for six months. EU students in Britain now pay international fees, and the EU wants them treated like domestic students. The food, carbon and youth deals are on hold until that issue is resolved, RTÉ reported.

The EU aims to begin applying the three agreements on March 1, 2028. The first young people could use the youth scheme by 2030.

## What Burnham said

"This is a good day for Britain and this is a good day for Europe," Burnham told reporters. He said the deals were probably the limit of what could be achieved in this Parliament. The next UK general election is due by mid-2029.

The House of Commons and House of Lords will now scrutinise the texts. The House of Lords Library has published a briefing on what each agreement covers.', 'Boston Daily Staff', 'https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Quartier_europ%C3%A9en_Bruxelles_2011-06.JPG/1280px-Quartier_europ%C3%A9en_Bruxelles_2011-06.JPG', 'The European Quarter in Brussels, home of the EU institutions (file photo). Photo by Zinneke via Wikimedia Commons (GFDL/CC BY-SA); editor to confirm the license on the file page', 'licensed', 'newsroom', 'content/2026-11-23-uk.sql', 8, 1, 'newsroom-assistant', '2026-11-24T01:45:00.000Z', '2026-11-24T01:45:00.000Z');
